### =====================================================================
### Detection rule triggers — dev testing
###
### Thresholds below match your rules as built:
###   BruteForceRule       threshold 10, window 10 min  (>=10 AUTH_FAILURE from one IP)
###   PasswordSprayingRule threshold 10, window 10 min  (>=10 DISTINCT principals from one IP)
###   KeyIdorRule          threshold 10, window 10 min  (>=10 AUTHZ_IDOR from one IP)
###
### If you change a rule's threshold(), change the repeat count here to match.
###
### ---------------------------------------------------------------------
### READ THIS BEFORE RUNNING — the CSRF behaviour that breaks the
### KEY_IDOR section:
###
### 1. This app ROTATES the CSRF token on every authenticated request. Each
###    response carries TWO Set-Cookie headers for XSRF-TOKEN: an expiry
###    (`XSRF-TOKEN=; Max-Age=0`) FIRST, then the replacement. So the value
###    that worked a moment ago is already dead, and a handler that grabs
###    the first match captures the EMPTY one. Every handler below therefore
###    re-captures, and requires a non-empty value.
###
### 2. Spring only emits Set-Cookie when it ISSUES a token. On a plain
###    /api/auth/login it stays silent, so the `if (c)` guards keep the last
###    good value rather than clobbering it with nothing.
###
### => Run this file TOP TO BOTTOM. Every request in the KEY_IDOR section
###    depends on the token handed back by the one before it.
### => Re-running only part of it? Clear the HTTP Client's cookies first
###    (the `.idea/httpRequests/http-client.cookies` file), then start
###    from the top.
###
### The handlers assert loudly, so a bad run fails on the spot instead of
### quietly 403-ing for the next 12 requests.
### ---------------------------------------------------------------------
###
### Host is pinned to 127.0.0.1, NOT localhost: `localhost` can resolve to
### the IPv6 loopback (::1) on macOS, and the rules group by source_ip. A
### client that resolves to ::1 lands in a DIFFERENT bucket than one that
### resolves to 127.0.0.1, so a split-stack run can leave both halves under
### the threshold and fire nothing.
### =====================================================================

@host = http://127.0.0.1:8080
@goodPassword = SuperSecret123
@wrongPassword = definitely-wrong-password


### =====================================================================
### SETUP — run once. Creates the victim + the attacker.
### The first request is also where the CSRF token gets captured.
### =====================================================================

### victim: owns the API key that gets probed in the KEY_IDOR section
POST {{host}}/api/auth/register
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "victim@test.local",
  "username": "victim_user",
  "displayName": "Victim User",
  "password": "{{goodPassword}}"
}

> {%
    // Capture on the FIRST request — a clean run is guaranteed to issue a
    // token here. Fires on 201 and on 409 (user already exists) alike.
    // The regex demands a non-empty value so the `XSRF-TOKEN=; Max-Age=0`
    // expiry header is skipped; it is emitted BEFORE the real one.
    const c = response.headers.valuesOf("Set-Cookie").find(h => /^XSRF-TOKEN=[^;]+/.test(h));
    if (c) client.global.set("xsrf", c.split(";")[0].split("=")[1]);

    client.test("CSRF token available", function () {
        const t = client.global.get("xsrf");
        client.assert(!!t, "No XSRF token. Clear the HTTP Client cookies and re-run from the top of this file.");
    });
%}

### attacker: does the probing
POST {{host}}/api/auth/register
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "attacker@test.local",
  "username": "attacker_user",
  "displayName": "Attacker User",
  "password": "{{goodPassword}}"
}


