### =====================================================================
### VERIFY — the part that actually proves a rule fired.
### Firing requests is not the test; a row in `alerts` is the test.
###
### Run in a terminal:
###
###   # events that landed (the rules' raw input)
###   docker exec secureapi-postgres psql -U secureapi -d secureapi -c \
###     "SELECT event_type, host(source_ip) AS ip, COUNT(*), COUNT(DISTINCT principal) AS distinct_principals
###      FROM security_events WHERE created_at > now() - interval '10 minutes'
###      GROUP BY event_type, source_ip ORDER BY 3 DESC;"
###
###   # alerts produced (the rules' output)
###   docker exec secureapi-postgres psql -U secureapi -d secureapi -c \
###     "SELECT id, rule_name, severity, fingerprint, created_at, suppress_until FROM alerts ORDER BY id DESC;"
###
### CHECK THE IP IN THE FINGERPRINT. Every entity should read 127.0.0.1.
### A `::1` means something reached the app over IPv6 and its events were
### counted as a separate attacker.
###
### SUPPRESSION CHECK — the one most people skip:
###   run a section TWICE, then confirm the alert count did NOT grow and
###   suppress_until moved forward. That proves the ON CONFLICT upsert works.
###
### Alert ids will show gaps (2 -> 121). That is normal: Postgres allocates
### a sequence value before testing the conflict, so every suppressed upsert
### burns an id. Gaps are not lost rows.
###
### NOTE ON TIMEZONES: psql via `docker exec` uses the container's UTC clock,
### but your app writes JVM-local (PDT) wall-clock times — a 7-hour gap.
### Use `now() - interval` comparisons (as above) rather than eyeballing
### timestamps, or rows will look hours old when they're seconds old.
###
### RESET between runs (optional):
###   docker exec secureapi-postgres psql -U secureapi -d secureapi -c \
###     "DELETE FROM alerts; DELETE FROM security_events;"
### =====================================================================
